{"tool_name":"admixtools","contents":["biocontainers","bioschemas","biotools","bioconda","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"admixtools","version":"8.0.2","home":"https://github.com/DReichLab/AdmixTools","documentation":"https://github.com/DReichLab/AdmixTools/blob/v8.0.2/README","license":"Academic License","summary":"ADMIXTOOLS (Patterson et al. 2012) is a software package that supports formal tests of whether admixture occurred, and makes it possible to infer admixture proportions and dates.","identifiers":["biotools:AdmixTools","doi:10.1534/genetics.112.145037"],"license_file":"src/LICENSE.txt","build":{"number":0,"run_exports":[null]},"requirements":{"build":["make",null],"host":["gsl","openblas","argp-standalone"],"run":["gsl","openblas","argp-standalone"]},"source_url":"https://github.com/DReichLab/AdmixTools/archive/refs/tags/v8.0.2.tar.gz","test":{"commands":["qp3Pop 2>&1 | grep Usage"]}},"biocontainers":{"name":"admixtools","identifiers":["biotools:AdmixTools","doi:10.1534/genetics.112.145037"],"license":"Free for Academic Use","summary":"ADMIXTOOLS (Patterson et al. 2012) is a software package that supports formal tests of whether admixture occurred, and makes it possible to infer admixture proportions and dates.","home_url":"https://github.com/DReichLab/AdmixTools","keywords":["Continental Population Groups","Ethnic Groups","Evolution, Molecular","Gene Flow","Genetics, Population","Humans","Hybridization, Genetic","Internet","Models, Genetic","Polymorphism, Single Nucleotide","Population genetics"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"admixtools","home":"https://github.com/DReichLab/AdmixTools","license":"Unlicense","summary":"Implements five methods to analyze admixture: the three-population test, D-statistics, F4-ratio estimation, admixture graph fitting, and rolloff. In addition, it computes lower and upper bounds on admixture proportions based on f3-statistics. Contains 6 programs: convertf, qp3Pop, qpBound, qpDstat, qpF4Ratio, and rolloff.","addition_date":"2017-08-20T14:56:58Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:36:47.815847Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"collections":["Animal and Crop Genomics"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:admixtools","credit":[{"email":"nickp@broadinstitute.org","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/DReichLab/AdmixTools"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["animalandcropgenomics"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Genetic variation analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3197"}]}],"name":"AdmixTools","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"admin","publication":[{"doi":"10.1534/genetics.112.145037","metadata":{"abstract":"Population mixture is an important process in biology. We present a suite of methods for learning about population mixtures, implemented in a software package called ADMIXTOOLS, that support formal tests for whether mixture occurred and make it possible to infer proportions and dates of mixture. We also describe the development of a new single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) array consisting of 629,433 sites with clearly documented ascertainment that was specifically designed for population genetic analyses and that we genotyped in 934 individuals from 53 diverse populations. To illustrate the methods, we give a number of examples that provide new insights about the history of human admixture. The most striking finding is a clear signal of admixture into northern Europe, with one ancestral population related to present-day Basques and Sardinians and the other related to present-day populations of northeast Asia and the Americas. This likely reflects a history of admixture between Neolithic migrants and the indigenous Mesolithic population of Europe, consistent with recent analyses of ancient bones from Sweden and the sequencing of the genome of the Tyrolean \"Iceman.\" \u00a9 2012 by the Genetics Society of America.","authors":[{"name":"Genschoreck T."},{"name":"Luo Y."},{"name":"Mallick S."},{"name":"Moorjani P."},{"name":"Patterson N."},{"name":"Reich D."},{"name":"Rohland N."},{"name":"Webster T."},{"name":"Zhan Y."}],"citationCount":1605,"date":"2012-11-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Genetics","title":"Ancient admixture in human history"},"pmcid":"PMC3522152","pmid":"22960212","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Population genetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3056"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"AdmixTools","home":"https://bio.tools/admixtools","license":"Unlicense","summary":"Implements five methods to analyze admixture: the three-population test, D-statistics, F4-ratio estimation, admixture graph fitting, and rolloff. In addition, it computes lower and upper bounds on admixture proportions based on f3-statistics. Contains 6 programs: convertf, qp3Pop, qpBound, qpDstat, qpF4Ratio, and rolloff.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:admixtools/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":true,"registration":false},"packages":true,"source_packages":true,"sourcecode":{"free":true,"interpreted":false,"license":false,"public":true,"readme":true,"repository":{"anonymous":true,"online":true}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":false},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":1518,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"-1"},{"count":3,"year":"2012"},{"count":16,"year":"2013"},{"count":33,"year":"2014"},{"count":44,"year":"2015"},{"count":64,"year":"2016"},{"count":80,"year":"2017"},{"count":91,"year":"2019"},{"count":93,"year":"2018"},{"count":111,"year":"2020"},{"count":140,"year":"2026"},{"count":155,"year":"2022"},{"count":155,"year":"2023"},{"count":163,"year":"2021"},{"count":173,"year":"2024"},{"count":196,"year":"2025